      "id": 1,
      "text": "Altona was originally called Modoc, then Ni Wot, before settling on Altona in 1879 to avoid postal confusion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/altona_colorado",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Altona Schoolhouse, built in 1880, served one-room grades one through eight and now hosts public programs on pioneer life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/altona_colorado",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Chief Niwot, a Southern Arapaho leader, welcomed early settlers in 1858–59, and the creek and the town’s name honor his legacy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/altona_colorado",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Loveland branch railroad reached the area in 1873, boosting transport for goods even though Altona never had a direct rail line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/altona_colorado",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Altona sits 10 miles west of Longmont along Nelson Road, within the Boulder County open‑space network.",
